WEBVTT

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.420
Where are you headed?

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:39.340
Do you think I could get a ride to the Basilica of St. Thérèse?

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:42.470
Sorry.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:49.890
Thank you.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.590
Feel free to have some of that juice.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:19.770
Thank you.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:28.720
It's really good.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.940
It's made from apples that have just been picked.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.900
Are you going to the Basilica to pray?

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.440
No, for work.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.280
Work?

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.990
You don't look like a sister to me.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.660
I'm actually going to be doing restoration work on the church.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:47.080
I thought Claude was in charge of the restoration work?

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.750
Do you know Claude?

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.620
I saw him a few times, back when he used to stay at the Basilica's asylum.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.170
That was a long time ago, though.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.460
The asylum?

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.590
Oh yeah, I heard there's an Asian woman staying there right now.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.470
I think she's been there for two years now.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.430
Apparently she locks herself in her room and just prays all day.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.600
Is that the Basilica of St. Thérèse?

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.440
It's the pride of our town.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.110
This is the first time I've seen it with my own eyes.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.780
Thank you for the ride.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.750
It's not everyday that I get to rescue a damsel in distress.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:34.160
I thought things were getting busy at the distillery?

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.250
I'm certain this is the last place you should be at right now.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.750
Yeah, yeah, I'm leaving.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.050
Why don't you swing by the distillery sometime?

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.840
I'll treat you to some Calvados.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.440
This place is so beautiful.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.810
I believe we've met before.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.820
My name is Yujin Jung. It's a pleasure to meet you.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:07.660
Sister Catherine?

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.910
Please show Miss Yujin to her room.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:13.410
Yes, Sister.

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.560
Sister Catherine will guide you around the Basilica

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.330
and explain the ground rules to you.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.050
This is a holy place.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.170
I hope you'll keep that in mind.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:28.760
I heard you were very skilled,

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:33.060
but we asked Claude to complete this job.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:38.690
To us, you're just a site foreman whom Claude's assigned to the Basilica.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.610
When will Claude get here?

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.110
He said he actually has a few projects going on at the moment,

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:47.610
and that he'll try to get here when he can.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.270
A lot of the gypsies have a tendency to pick pockets on trains.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:11.640
You should be more careful.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:16.270
At least I got here in one piece, right?

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:17.730
True.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.400
Oh, did Claude actually say that he'd come here?

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:25.400
Wait, don't you know?

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.890
You don't know why the Reverend Mother's so determined

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.780
to have Claude do the restoration?

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:33.950
Why?

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:39.210
I can't really separate rumor from truth,

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:42.130
but you've heard about the Miracle of the Basilica, right?

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.040
The Miracle of the Basilica?

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.450
It's said that fervent believers who used to pray here a long time ago

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:53.010
were cured of blindness and other incurable diseases.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.220
I've heard of that, yeah.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.430
Well, apparently there's more to it than that.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.790
<i>It was twelve years ago.</i>

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.960
<i>An architect who suddenly lost his vision</i>

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:30.260
<i>was being nursed here.</i>

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:34.390
<i>He fell in love with the young woman who took care of him.</i>

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.800
<i>He neither believed in the Miracle of the Basilica,</i>

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.800
<i>nor thought that he ever had a chance at life again.</i>

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.980
<i>However, the woman who loved him thought differently.</i>

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:58.200
<i>Her fervent prayers must have touched the heart of the Lord,</i>

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.250
and miraculously, he was able to see again.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:08.210
But the young architect, even though his vision had been restored,

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:11.590
couldn't find her, no matter where he looked.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:17.640
Nor did he ever find a shred of evidence that the woman actually existed.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:29.820
So what happened, exactly?

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.130
Some people say that the woman who prayed for the young architect,

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:37.530
and nursed him back to health,

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:40.580
is the statue right here.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.040
No way, that's impossible.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.370
That's why it's called a miracle.

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:50.630
Was the young architect in that story Claude, by any chance?

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:52.050
That's one thing we know for sure.

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:57.010
I guess I did hear that he used to stay at the asylum.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.000
I hope he really comes here.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.350
I have so many things I want to ask.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.100
Wow! You can see the entire village from here.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:06.810
Which one's the distillery?

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.320
You see the series of houses over there?

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.610
Yeah, I see them.

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.910
That's the Calvados distillery.

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:18.700
Now is the busiest time of their year,

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.120
because they just finished picking the apples.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.570
Oh yeah, the Calvados festival isn't far off.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:23.700
There's a festival?

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:27.710
That's why so many gypsies and tourists have been coming here lately.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.090
There'll be plenty to see and lots to eat.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:32.340
Let's go see it together when it happens.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:34.840
Yeah, sounds like fun.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.010
It'll be grand.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:39.930
Why don't we head to the office now?

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:40.680
Sure.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.610
You may leave now, Sister Catherine.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:53.270
Yes, Sister.

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:00.160
This room used to be part of the asylum.

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.620
It should serve as a good office because it's quiet.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.290
The asylum, you say?

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.290
It closed down a long time ago.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.790
But there's still one person whom we're nursing here,

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:14.960
so I hope you won't bother her, if possible.

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:16.770
She's in the room at the end of the hall on the second floor,

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:18.630
so you shouldn't run into her either.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:21.140
Is she Asian by any chance?

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:25.770
Sounds like somebody's been talking out of turn again.

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:29.140
Oh no, I just thought I heard someone mention it before.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.310
This is a small village.

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:34.570
Word travels fast here. Please be careful with what you say.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:36.150
Yes, I understand.

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.820
We're also planning on remodeling and reopening the asylum

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:43.120
once the restoration of the Basilica is finished.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.870
I'll take care of that too.

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:52.420
Oh, I meant <i>we</i> — <i>we</i> will take care of this place too.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:57.420
Claude also asked me to consider taking on the remodeling.

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.180
You're not very good at lying, I see.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:01.680
Excuse me?

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:05.010
I know that Claude sent you here because he didn't want to

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:08.600
accept the responsibility for restoring the Basilica himself.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:11.880
Despite that, I have a good reason

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.690
for letting you work on the restoration.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:16.020
And what is that reason?

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:20.320
I figure you have to be someone trustworthy

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.910
if Claude was willing to lend you his name and send you out here.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.240
Also, if even a single problem or issue arises,

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.830
Claude will be held responsible, not you.

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.540
He won't easily be able to abandon his responsibility when that happens.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.130
So basically you have nothing to lose by letting me work.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:42.220
The Basilica isn't merely a collection of bricks, you know.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:45.260
It's my duty to protect the sanctity of this place.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:49.100
It seems we've been talking for a long time.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:50.810
When will the workers get here?

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.140
They should arrive soon.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.810
Then thank you in advance for your hard work.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.730
I'm sorry. I just can't stop laughing.

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:57.350
You scared the living daylights out of me.

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:01.110
I thought you'd be hungry since I didn't see you at dinner.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:03.400
I forgot about dinner.

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:07.030
Does she cry a lot in the middle of the night?

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.070
I don't really know.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:11.910
You have no idea how scary it was.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:14.540
Oh yeah, could you take a look at this for me?

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.500
I found it inside the cabinet over there.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.120
It's a record of all the people who worked in,

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.050
and were admitted to, the asylum.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:28.130
I called the office and asked around a bit,

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:32.970
and apparently 1990 was when Claude stopped working for a year.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:36.680
The Calvados we had was a 1990 vintage as well.

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:40.560
He even said that there was "more inside the bottle than just brandy."

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:43.590
A 1990 vintage of Calvados?

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:44.570
"More than just brandy"?

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:46.400
It's a long story.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:50.360
Take a look.

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:52.410
Apparently, there were only six nurses in the asylum that year,

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:54.030
and they were in their twenties.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.370
You want to find her yourself?

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:00.580
Claude helped me a lot.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:04.630
It's thanks to him that I'm working on this project.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.340
What will you do after you find her?

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:07.550
I don't know.

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:10.720
I guess I'll think about that when it happens.

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:28.830
Let me go!

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:29.860
What's your problem?

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:31.820
Get your ass out here!

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:34.530
We don't need your kind around here.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:41.660
It's a mistake!

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:44.670
You still think it's a mistake?

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.960
I took pity on you and let you work here,

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:52.630
and you repay that favor by slacking off and stealing our merchandise?

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:55.130
You rat bastard.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.060
Were you going to sell those to someone?

00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:58.310
I wasn't stealing it.

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.230
The distillery was going to throw it away anyway.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.900
Why does it matter what happens to it?

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:07.400
Would you listen to this kid?

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.250
Fine, I'll quit.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:11.360
You happy?

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:13.820
I guess it was my fault

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:17.740
for hiring a rootless gypsy punk like you.

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:22.370
What?

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:23.790
I'm a rootless gypsy punk, you say?

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:25.630
You little punk!

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:27.670
Would you two stop it?

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:32.260
Thank God you're here.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:37.970
This gypsy punk's been stealing our juice!

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:39.890
Look in his bag!

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:41.930
Can you believe this?

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:43.730
Please.

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:47.310
We weren't planning on reporting it to the police.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.230
We wouldn't be doing ourselves any favors

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:53.190
by starting trouble with the gypsies right before the festival, either.

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:56.470
I only came by to say thank you for yesterday,

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:57.700
but it looks like you've done me a favor again.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:16:59.910
Oh no, I've done no such thing.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:06.210
You can go now.

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:10.170
I'm letting you off the hook this time because of the mademoiselle here.

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:32.900
Here.

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:35.650
Did you...

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:37.910
I bought it with half a day's wages.

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:39.980
You don't have to drink it if you don't want to,

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:41.660
but I consider my debt to you repaid.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:47.920
Come on, I'll give you back your bag.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:52.460
Don't people usually say "I'm sorry" or "Please forgive me" first?

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.200
I didn't take anything.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:58.030
You were only carrying checks and had no cash,

00:17:58.030 --> 00:17:59.800
so I was going to give it back to you anyway.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:01.800
I trust you'll pay me the commission?

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:03.510
What commission?

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:06.560
I found something that you lost.

00:18:08.270 --> 00:18:09.270
You win.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:14.440
Let me know if there's anything else you want me to find for you.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:17.280
You'll have to pay me commission though.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:19.720
What about people?

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.080
Could you find lost people for me as well?

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:23.280
What?

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.620
You'll be paid a commission for it, of course.

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:27.290
How about it? Want to give it a try?

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:29.420
Okay.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:32.670
Here.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:37.930
It sounds like you're satisfied with her work,

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:40.470
judging by the fact that you haven't complained about anything.

00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:47.310
Don't worry. I won't ever have to go to the Basilica myself.

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:50.020
Say "hi" to Yujin for me.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:02.530
Joan...

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:06.240
You're not expecting me to actually show up, are you?

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:09.910
"Morosov said, as he pointed to the wooden Madonna,

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:13.250
'This isn't a thank you, it's a cry for help.'" {he's quoting Erich Maria Remarque's Arch of Triumph}

00:19:15.310 --> 00:19:18.550
I lived, yet you disappeared.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:25.680
You're looking for me?

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:29.280
You worked at the asylum in the Basilica of St. Thérèse fourteen years ago,

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.140
didn't you?

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:32.140
I did. Why?

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:34.610
Do you remember a patient named Claude?

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:38.650
He's around your age, and he was an architect.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:41.650
I can't say I do.

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:44.310
He couldn't see while he was at the asylum,

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:46.160
but he regained his vision after undergoing surgery.

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:48.160
Are you sure you don't remember him?

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:54.420
Sorry I couldn't help you.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:01.420
Have you kept in touch with your coworker, Marianne, from the asylum?

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:05.890
Oh, Marianne works here with me.

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:08.390
Could I meet her right now?

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:12.390
She's not on call today, but she should be here tomorrow.

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:13.520
Thank you.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:21.690
Marianne's the only one left on the list now.

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:25.190
Marianne might just be the heroine we're looking for.

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:28.200
You're really optimistic, you know that?

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:31.010
Au contraire, this is all mathematical.

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:32.080
If you calculate the statistical probability...

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:33.350
You can stop right there.

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:34.710
All this math is giving me a headache.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:36.830
Can I sleep here tonight?

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:37.710
Again?

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.420
I can't read at the campsite 'cause it's too dark.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:44.550
Let me guess. You're reading "Arc de Triomphe" again, right?

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:50.430
Geez, you're going to memorize the entire book at this rate.

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:53.480
My father liked it.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:56.850
Apparently, he used to ask my mom to read it to him everyday.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:00.440
Don't you miss your dad?

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:02.940
You think I'm a kid or something?

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:06.740
I may be an adult, but I still miss my dad.

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:10.660
You can't exactly miss someone you've never met.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:17.250
I don't like the look you're giving me right now.

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:19.130
Sorry.

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:23.050
Yeah, you can go ahead and stay here tonight because it's late.

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:25.260
But be careful, you hear?

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:26.380
I will.

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:33.470
If Marianne isn't the woman who nursed Claude,

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.020
then it has to have been a miracle, right?

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:39.690
Will you really believe in it even if Marianne isn't the one?

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:40.770
In the miracle?

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:42.480
Yeah.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.910
The story of Claude and the woman who nursed him might not be a miracle.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:54.410
Even the Miracle of the Basilica might not be true.

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:56.290
Are you sure it's okay to deny a miracle like that?

00:21:56.830 --> 00:21:58.170
I'm still only a sister in training, you know.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.130
My faith is still lacking in many parts.

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:02.500
But I believe that miracles do happen.

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:07.430
My father liked raising flowers.

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:11.550
Whenever he found a new seed, he'd plant it in the garden

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:13.890
and tell me what kind of flower it would become.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:17.440
I'd forget all about it, but one day

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:20.480
a flower appeared that was more beautiful than my father had described.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:25.190
I saw that flower blooming in our garden.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:26.820
Yeah.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.710
Almost as if someone had planted a flower that was already blooming

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:31.450
in your garden, right?

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:37.540
I think little things like that, which happen all around us,

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:38.830
are miracles.

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:40.900
Becoming friends,

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:42.500
falling in love,

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:43.850
becoming someone's mother...

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:47.340
Who would ever have thought that I'd become a nun?

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:51.050
I think you were born to be a nun.

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:52.720
You think so?

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:53.720
Wait a second.

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:54.470
Huh?

00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:57.310
Do you hear that?

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:07.030
Louis!
